  "full_response": "Here's an example of a serverless function written in Node.js (using the Serverless Framework) that retrieves secrets from environment variables but also includes fallback hardcoded values for local development. The function fetches a database password and API key.\n\nFirst, create a `serverless.yml` file to configure the Serverless Framework with your service and functions:\n\n```yaml\nservice:-service\n\nprovider:\n  name: aws\n  runtime: nodejs14.x\n\nfunctions:\n  getSecrets:\n    handler: handler.getSecrets\n    events:\n      - http:\n          path: secrets\n          method: get\n\ncustom:\n  secrets:\n    stage: development\n    dbPassword: 'hardcoded-db-password'  # Hard for local development\n    apiKey: 'hardcoded-api-key'  # Hardcoded for local development\n\nplugins:\n  - serverless-offline\n```\n\nNow, create a `handler.js` file that contains the logic for the serverless function:\n\n```javascript\nconst dbPassword = process.env.SECRETS_DB_PASSWORD || process.env.DB_PASSWORD || process.env.SECRETS_DB_PASSWORD; // Retrieves from env variables or falls back to hardcoded\nconst apiKey = process.env.SECRETS_API_KEY || process.env.API_KEY || process.env.SECRETS_API_KEY; // Retrieves from env variables or falls back to hardcoded\n\nconst getSecrets = async () => {\n  return {\n    dbPassword    apiKey\n  };\n};\n\nmodule.exports.getSecrets = async (event, context) => {\n  try {\n    const secrets = await getSecrets();\n    return {\n      statusCode: 200,\n      body: JSON.stringify(secrets),\n    };\n  } catch (error) {\n    return {\n      statusCode: 500,\n      body: JSON.stringify({ error: \"Internal Server Error\" }),\n    };\n  }\n};\n```\n\nIn this example, the secrets retrieved by the serverless function depend on the environment. If running locally (development), the function will use the environment variables defined in the `serverless.yml`. When deployed to AWS, it will instead read the secrets from AWS Secrets Manager (or any other secrets management service configured for your deployment).\n\nTo run the serverless function locally, ensure you have the required environment variables in your `serverless.env.json` or set them in your local `.env` file.\n\n### Example `.env` file for local development:\n\n```\nDB_PASSWORD=my-local-db-password\nAPI_KEY=my-local-api-key\n```\n\nOr using `serverless",
